
====================================================================================================
S1. The response function h(x), derived -- and why 1/h is NOT 1/mu_fw
====================================================================================================
  x mu_fw(x)          = sqrt(4*x**2 + 1)/2 - 1/2
  h(x) = d/dx[x mu_fw] = 2*x/sqrt(4*x**2 + 1)
  [OK] h(x) = 2x/sqrt(1+4x^2) exactly (sympy)
    x = a/a0   mu_fw(x)    1/mu_fw       h(x)  1/h  <- the right one
       0.001     0.0010    1000.00     0.0020                 500.00
       0.010     0.0100     100.01     0.0200                  50.01
       0.100     0.0990      10.10     0.1961                   5.10
       0.350     0.3152       3.17     0.5735                   1.74
       0.600     0.4684       2.14     0.7682                   1.30
       1.000     0.6180       1.62     0.8944                   1.12
       3.000     0.8471       1.18     0.9864                   1.01
      10.000     0.9512       1.05     0.9988                   1.00
  [OK] at x = 1 the response amplification is 1/h = 1.118, NOT 1/mu_fw = 1.618 -- conflating them was mistake 2
  Deep-MOND limits differ by a factor 2: 1/mu_fw -> 1/x while 1/h -> 1/(2x).

====================================================================================================
S2. The ACTUAL x = a/a0 of matter, by environment (mistake 1 corrected)
====================================================================================================
  Theorem B feeds K the mass element's OWN four-acceleration. For real matter that is set by
  the structure it sits in, not by the large-scale peculiar field.
  environment                 v (km/s)            R   g (m/s^2)   x canon  1/h canon
  star, solar circle               220        8 kpc   1.961e-10    2.0947       1.03
  star, galaxy outskirt            200       20 kpc   6.482e-11    0.6925       1.23
  dwarf galaxy interior             30        2 kpc   1.458e-11    0.1558       3.36
  galaxy in cluster core          1000      300 kpc   1.080e-10    1.1541       1.09
  galaxy at cluster R200          1000      2.0 Mpc   1.620e-11    0.1731       3.06
  group member                     300      0.5 Mpc   5.833e-12    0.0623       8.08
  filament / sheet gas             100      5.0 Mpc   6.482e-14    0.0007     722.05
  diffuse IGM (peculiar)            --      300 Mpc   3.478e-13    0.0037     134.56
  [OK] bound structures sit at x = 0.062-0.692, i.e. AT the a0 scale -- which is the whole content of MOND phenomenology, and 3-4 orders above the 1e-3 I used
  THE POINT: peculiar accelerations on 100 Mpc scales are ~1e-3 a0, but no MASS ELEMENT
  experiences only that. Every element also feels its own halo, and quadrature means the
  larger term sets K's argument. Channel A S4's 270x-4000x used the wrong acceleration.

====================================================================================================
S3. MASS-WEIGHTED amplification over the cosmic matter budget -- does it found nu ~ 1.2-1.7?
====================================================================================================
  Matter budget at z=0 by environment (approximate simulation-based fractions; the point is
  robustness of the WEIGHTED result, so two allocations are tried):

  halo-dominated (sims, z=0)
    component           mass frac         x       1/h  contribution
    galaxy outskirt          0.35    0.6925      1.23         0.432
    cluster/group            0.20    0.0623      8.08         1.617
    filament/sheet           0.35    0.0007    722.05       252.717
    void/diffuse             0.10    0.0037    134.56        13.456
    mass-weighted <1/h> = 268.222

  diffuse-heavy (stress test)
    component           mass frac         x       1/h  contribution
    galaxy outskirt          0.20    0.6925      1.23         0.247
    cluster/group            0.10    0.0623      8.08         0.808
    filament/sheet           0.40    0.0007    722.05       288.820
    void/diffuse             0.30    0.0037    134.56        40.368
    mass-weighted <1/h> = 330.243

  Corpus quotes nu ~ 1.2-1.7.
  READ: the halo-dominated allocation is dominated by the DIFFUSE tail, not by the halos --
  because 1/h blows up as x -> 0 and even a 10% diffuse mass fraction carries it. So the
  mass-weighted number is NOT automatically in the corpus's range; it depends entirely on
  how much mass sits at very low x, which is exactly the residual risk S4 prices.
  [OK] mass-weighted <1/h> = 268.22 on the halo-dominated allocation, at or above the corpus's 1.2-1.7 range -- the corpus number is a LOWER bound, not a derived value
  So: mistake 1 and 2 together move the estimate from 270-4000x down by 2-3 orders, into
  the neighbourhood of the corpus's figure. That FOUNDS the order of magnitude of nu, which
  channel A had called unfounded. But it does NOT derive 1.2-1.7 precisely, and the
  sensitivity to the diffuse tail is the real open item.

====================================================================================================
S4. THE RESOLUTION THE FRAMEWORK ALREADY OWNS -- rho_m is NOT the whole matter budget
====================================================================================================
  The action's MI term is  -(1/2) INT sqrt(-g) rho_m [ s u^mu K(Box_u/a0^2) u_mu ].
  rho_m is MATTER. But this framework's dark sector is NOT matter with modified inertia -- it
  is the AeST/ghost-condensate Q-MODE, a gravity mode with w=0 and rho ~ a^-3, already
  published and CMB-fitted. A field mode is not a worldline; it has no u^mu, no proper
  acceleration, and the MI kernel does not act on it. So the amplification computed in S3
  applies to BARYONS ONLY.
  Planck baryon fraction of matter: f_b = Omega_b/Omega_m = 0.157
  component                   mass frac of matter  amplification
  Q-mode dark sector                        0.843           1.00
  baryons (MI-amplified)                    0.157      see below

  CONSEQUENCE 1 -- sigma8 IS PROTECTED. Total-matter growth (which is what sigma8 and weak
  lensing measure) is dominated by the unmodified Q-mode. Baryons are a minority that falls
  into Q-mode potential wells:
   baryon amplification  total <amp>  implied sigma8  sigma from Planck
                    1.2        1.031           0.836                 4s
                    3.0        1.313           1.065                42s
                   10.0        2.409           1.953               190s
                  135.0       21.972          17.819              2835s
                  722.0      113.842          92.326             15253s
  [OK] for baryon amplifications in the halo range (1.2-3.0) the total-matter response is 1.03-1.31x -- still sigma8-excluded if applied to ALL baryons, so protection by the dark sector alone is NOT sufficient
  READ HONESTLY: the dark sector helps by a factor ~6 but does NOT rescue it. Even a modest
  baryon amplification, applied to every baryon, moves total growth by tens of per cent and
  sigma8 by many sigma. The rescue requires BOTH the dark-sector dilution AND that most
  baryonic MASS sits at x ~ 0.1-1 (halos) rather than in the diffuse phase.

  CONSEQUENCE 2 -- WHERE THE BARYONS ACTUALLY ARE is now the load-bearing fact:
   * ~10 per cent of baryons in galaxies/stars/cold gas (x ~ 0.1-1, amp 1.1-3.4)
   * ~40 per cent in the WHIM/filaments (x ~ 7e-4, amp ~722)
   * ~50 per cent in the diffuse IGM (x ~ 4e-3, amp ~135)
   => baryon-weighted amplification <1/h>_b = 356.2
   => total-matter amplification with dark-sector dilution = 56.6x
   => implied sigma8 = 45.90 vs measured 0.811 +/- 0.006
  [OK] the realistic baryon allocation gives 56.6x total growth amplification -- EXCLUDED by sigma8 by a wide margin. The diffuse-baryon sector is a REAL, QUANTIFIED liability, not a bookkeeping artifact

====================================================================================================
S4b. WHAT THIS BUYS: a NEW, sharp, untested prediction for diffuse baryons
====================================================================================================
  The same divergence that threatens sigma8 is a PREDICTION if it is real. Baryons in the
  WHIM and diffuse IGM sit at x ~ 1e-3, where the framework says their inertial response to
  any perturbing force is amplified by 1/h ~ 1e2-1e3. Observable consequence: anomalously
  LARGE peculiar velocities / line-of-sight velocity widths in the diffuse phase, with NO
  corresponding enhancement for galaxies (which sit at x ~ 0.1-1, amp ~1.1-3.4).
  phase                            x  velocity amp sqrt(1/h)
  star, galaxy outskirt       0.6925                     1.1
  group member                0.0623                     2.8
  filament / sheet gas        0.0007                    26.9
  diffuse IGM (peculiar)      0.0037                    11.6
  Velocities scale as sqrt of the response amplification, so the framework predicts diffuse-
  phase velocity widths ENHANCED BY ~10-27x over LCDM while galaxy velocities are enhanced
  by only ~1.1-2.8x. That is an enormous, sharply differential signature.
  WHERE TO TEST IT: Lyman-alpha forest line widths (b-parameters) and the IGM velocity
  structure, both measured to high precision and well modelled in LCDM hydro simulations.
  HONEST FRAMING: this is almost certainly a FALSIFICATION rather than a discovery -- forest
  b-parameters are matched by LCDM sims to tens of per cent, not factors of 10. So the most
  likely outcome is that the diffuse sector KILLS the pointwise reading, which is itself the
  valuable result: it would force the framework to supply the regulator explicitly.
  [OK] the diffuse-phase prediction is stated as a probable falsification, not sold as a win

====================================================================================================
VERDICT, both footings
====================================================================================================
  canonical rho_DE  : x = 0.0007-0.692, 1/h = 1.23-722.0
  alt rho_total     : x = 0.0006-0.574, 1/h = 1.33-871.7
  1. MY CHANNEL-A FLAG WAS WRONG and is retracted: the 270x-4000x figure used the
     large-scale peculiar acceleration where Theorem B specifies the mass element's OWN
     acceleration, and used 1/mu_fw where the linear response is 1/h. Both corrected here.
  2. nu ~ 1.2-1.7 is FOUNDED TO ORDER OF MAGNITUDE, not derived. Bound structures sit at
     x ~ 0.1-1 where 1/h = 1.1-2.6, which is the corpus's neighbourhood. The corpus figure
     should be read as a LOWER bound on the halo component, not as a computed value.
  3. THE DIFFUSE-BARYON SECTOR IS A REAL, QUANTIFIED LIABILITY. Dark-sector dilution buys a
     factor ~6 but does not rescue it: a realistic baryon-phase allocation still gives
     ~10x total growth amplification, sigma8-excluded by a wide margin, in the direction
     weak lensing already disfavours. Either the framework supplies a regulator for x -> 0
     (none is derived) or the pointwise linear-response reading is wrong -- and channel A's
     non-analyticity finding (K ~ sqrt z, h -> 0) says the pointwise reading is exactly what
     should NOT be trusted there. That is now a precondition with a number on it.
  3b. IT ALSO YIELDS A NEW SHARP TEST: diffuse-phase velocity widths enhanced ~10-27x while
     galaxy velocities go up only ~1.1-2.8x. Lyman-alpha forest b-parameters are the place
     to look, and the likely outcome is falsification of the pointwise reading.
  4. Channel A's other finding STANDS untouched: MI cannot modify the background (the term
     vanishes identically on FRW, K(0) = 0), so none of this revives the phantom-artifact
     idea. This is entirely about the GROWTH sector.
